#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124304 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "4c1e01c1-d607-5ad8-8643-b3317201ab9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4832" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2\\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ If $\\mathbf{c}_1=\\mathbf{b}_2-4\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2=5\\mathbf{b}_1+7\\mathbf{b}_2,$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are\n![](images/da/daf4111656a525abf51bf58105831ed11378ed7fc52915e1e58ca5f75884bcf0.svg)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/be/bed6471ce9aed8eb16a0d35f1d9985b8e99941f7c1326645de971960bdb5aedd.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124305 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "93b466b2-9b8d-55fa-87b8-b5bf24a497cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-4833" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Find the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n![](images/95/957c59d27a871a52f110c3dca91cec390091c13ceba6e14d6485f92aa150c51a.svg)\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is \n\n![](images/60/6095f1a65a9b9b245de2dd4cad611d2616a2b3b15e4701fe33c3c53b913c2283.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consists of the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2,$ and $\\mathbf e_3,$ given by\n![](images/41/41bd0d4956d8ec09d0339a79329c6739e07e698d9dee9f192968d77c9ec28842.svg)\n\nThe components of the vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n![](images/30/30a1bdc2316f5d7acc527cb1d69fc11c7b732f9e22a4d57a11ed84f2db1e3cd5.svg)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/56/5606e00ccc39829e3c599aa86286dcc1f1cb4e3febb280adf4e1bbe18dc7ab6a.svg)\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=0,$ and $c=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124306 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "6c5b5534-2082-593e-af07-df83497ea84c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4835" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by \n![](images/46/4676de86bd5b4bc2a8e1d6a9432379d0769163113913137f8143a71eb16e8240.svg)\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 5-(-6)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124307 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "0929855b-0310-5c87-8cff-cc2faa8a5ae0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4837" :question/problem "Let ![](images/07/076634439afec4c0e4fb0a443d88163245269988e323ddb9d83d1a4a1b18139e.svg) and ![](images/b2/b2fd30034f45fb2d8b06f2697f137c93de21942822b08193c3e640ef51d4e7c0.svg) be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/dd/dd733ebebc7131eb2762e6e98e1d14d07a730e5e37657c639ae9cc46be123ee2.svg)\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}= & [\\begin{aligned}\\,4 & 22 \\\\ \\,1 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b7ac1d-4efd-51c0-b67d-14536d1d6f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416524} {:answer/id #uuid "59e9f366-67f4-5425-be8d-c3bdec30dc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416547} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8542ed-fbae-5713-bdec-3842f4a51f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416561} {:answer/id #uuid "e96a7aec-7264-57b6-b1e0-4a5a84fb5122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416580} {:answer/id #uuid "f19d556d-427e-5f81-8e9f-38999a23661f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02b7ac1d-4efd-51c0-b67d-14536d1d6f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416524} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddc74432-9277-50d2-8b8f-c46b82fbfa4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8443228-62d0-53de-820f-4c2c2789eb2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-113104" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ from $B$ to $C$ given that\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c074f1f1-190d-5c2b-be44-47f1519f9e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386642} {:answer/id #uuid "428736dc-252f-56df-b2cf-058df8e2f2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386643} {:answer/id #uuid "4293ab9b-7451-59be-b692-d3049e359a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386644} {:answer/id #uuid "a593476a-084b-51fd-b813-f788df67a1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386645} {:answer/id #uuid "dba8598e-a9a8-5938-a8b8-24e380b6f380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a593476a-084b-51fd-b813-f788df67a1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386645} :answer-field/id #uuid "570439f3-213e-5838-a79e-eae761e0d96a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bcd060e-67aa-5a69-a9d1-f310cdace57a" :question/math-academy-id "q-113105" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ If ${b}_{1}=2{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ from $B$ to $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07838b3a-5d21-5d4b-96ed-ad6dca8941c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416525} {:answer/id #uuid "16853ab7-760d-5058-8e30-4207c809785e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416528} {:answer/id #uuid "87e78e9b-fea5-57d2-9ca3-96814468f5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416560} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b03fb3-ebd1-50f3-8cee-c5c60249d1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416573} {:answer/id #uuid "c80a8828-4df2-559c-b9ff-6f138f31421c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16853ab7-760d-5058-8e30-4207c809785e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416528} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c86502d-b8b8-5473-bf1a-5d435069f0e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6ffc405-f81c-5336-ad94-25ea55190ac1" :question/math-academy-id "q-113342" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a7deef8-19c9-5a99-beb9-30ea6eec4a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416535} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6f0908-6c0d-5c24-87f5-821d61c33178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416540} {:answer/id #uuid "90c78f39-cbdd-5735-93c9-853cf06b1825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416563} {:answer/id #uuid "aa836202-b0e6-521c-8443-a083a03ee71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416568} {:answer/id #uuid "b249e81c-9b0f-550d-b583-627092bcf567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90c78f39-cbdd-5735-93c9-853cf06b1825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416563} :answer-field/id #uuid "04d718c2-a214-5ef1-82b5-17f86c63db92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a26dc326-54d9-5ae2-96c3-423118f36c64" :question/math-academy-id "q-113409" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60bc38dd-e04b-53b1-91b2-6bb378d41837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416549} {:answer/id #uuid "71d81bba-103c-52d1-9ba7-ba524273847c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416555} {:answer/id #uuid "7fde4386-2338-52c7-a292-c73f0e034d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416559} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf2c82b-0f69-5967-94d6-03180ca54721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416565} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7a94f0-7d2a-56ba-bb59-168b33d3bb36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fde4386-2338-52c7-a292-c73f0e034d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416559} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cc46cdd-beb2-5ee4-a2a8-1259b1fb90ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df33f267-a622-55d4-b6ca-07fa624cf0e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-113410" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "919eb1e9-2f4b-56b6-88e5-3859874737bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386677} {:answer/id #uuid "90eeb8b6-e3b5-53db-a5fe-f9746d4ccb88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386678} {:answer/id #uuid "b872cd7b-0659-5c54-8111-e27c28adc0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386679} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdc24c8-890c-5fc5-a78e-92d48b19a269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386680} {:answer/id #uuid "e9bec6ef-0b83-555d-8bfe-9ff77d315986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "919eb1e9-2f4b-56b6-88e5-3859874737bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386677} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f7670fd-c5bc-51f7-98ba-bd3ac9cdc072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e492abb-150e-5b29-ab40-6db339f73d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113415" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40f45c93-6fe2-5f26-851c-9bd122ebd2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416541} {:answer/id #uuid "8fca4fa8-84ec-5284-b191-c15918cd020b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416562} {:answer/id #uuid "b825f629-82ac-53f3-b87f-12a36ddac7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416572} {:answer/id #uuid "caa59a06-8d44-5e97-8650-fe3fd7d19fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416577} {:answer/id #uuid "f89b032a-28b2-5d9f-9d76-0bd18854f341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f89b032a-28b2-5d9f-9d76-0bd18854f341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416583} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a77bdea-a6bc-5acf-8e6b-c3386884ffde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "699ab275-8547-554b-97d2-1ddcd2846071" :question/math-academy-id "q-113508" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}$ and $C={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1100f1bc-ed34-5a67-be8b-4cd9cbd023d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416527} {:answer/id #uuid "50b04112-6a4b-5de0-8b36-cac7c5a0a40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416544} {:answer/id #uuid "5a88c79a-d8b2-5e43-8a47-5013ebd4e46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416548} {:answer/id #uuid "945a35b8-1727-56f0-8d1f-a7a7ff35591a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416564} {:answer/id #uuid "a2edcdc2-4bd9-58b5-8e0b-973a0e0009a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1100f1bc-ed34-5a67-be8b-4cd9cbd023d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416527} :answer-field/id #uuid "08ec3541-b791-5354-9296-f775e0ff2635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f239f11-8e8b-53a2-a96f-22cc233efad1" :question/math-academy-id "q-113526" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20235027-ad55-59fe-b1e5-b1705f888d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416531} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd79918-775f-5123-b336-42017be04695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416543} {:answer/id #uuid "7928c32a-4f5a-5fbd-ab9a-645a98853355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416556} {:answer/id #uuid "7c7fa779-b799-54a8-ad4c-a9e9dcea93d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416558} {:answer/id #uuid "ead71fbb-62b9-559f-94fe-b5cd728b52ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dd79918-775f-5123-b336-42017be04695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416543} :answer-field/id #uuid "249ce60b-00ae-5912-900c-fca2a8f69bfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f27d85cd-7d1d-52da-929b-4fa8b04cc954" :question/math-academy-id "q-35805" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2344242d-6b96-5993-b8b0-de107b4279a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=4,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416533} {:answer/id #uuid "3879c262-51c9-5503-937f-270c4a66ac2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=4,$ $b=1,$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416537} {:answer/id #uuid "54569e6c-7221-5713-add5-c98abf257a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416545} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c37f7a-6b0a-5951-912a-c0791c4bd8ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=0,$ and $c=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416567} {:answer/id #uuid "b19c2423-673d-5c2f-9566-5866776b9ddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2344242d-6b96-5993-b8b0-de107b4279a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=4,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416533} :answer-field/id #uuid "4928869d-0a7f-5e17-a81c-6aaf1def0a0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30d93e6c-378b-5de5-9291-694547fd0977" :question/math-academy-id "q-36354" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}.$ \nWhat are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & =2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{2} & =4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{3} & =2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a & 2 \\\\ b & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & c & -3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=4,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b26590d-792e-51f9-b6ee-9b92d34732ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416538} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3ac9c4-89a8-53e0-9f5b-0281ad0e261f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416539} {:answer/id #uuid "419a7dcc-0ec9-5201-aaac-a0d26fd4a862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416542} {:answer/id #uuid "57bd0aaf-7e37-5d1f-aa28-5056aba60ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416546} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f96822-b423-5c09-9a06-dd548ce5f549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" 