\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{ and }\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "520cf59b-0e8a-5135-a5bb-58dbfaac9d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=0,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349192} {:answer/id #uuid "63a6a38a-272b-5ef8-ac5d-9b8a62db6e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=0,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349359} {:answer/id #uuid "a929d2cb-f110-55b5-829c-d6e3d6ec6f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349954} {:answer/id #uuid "b73754bf-f54b-5788-b870-664463b6849c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350069} {:answer/id #uuid "efee334f-bad5-5592-a240-43676034e441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efee334f-bad5-5592-a240-43676034e441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350545} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f8f9c2f-e58d-54c9-bff6-90fe41f9bb30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ff70514-6088-5578-a352-2d83d0571687" :question/math-academy-id "q-81394" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}.$ \nWhat are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}-{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{2} & =5{c}_{3}+2{c}_{2}, \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={c}_{2}-{c}_{1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ a & b & 1 \\\\ -1 & c & 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41e4b858-0939-54cf-9dda-08263bf19bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349044} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4ebaf4-f6ae-5d83-b8f7-d86c8df809a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=0,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349136} {:answer/id #uuid "677d8855-5a11-57ef-aca3-3b105f83a4d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349398} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffbd4c9-7f71-5f70-aa18-f035562c2e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=4,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349475} {:answer/id #uuid "8948bc51-f3d5-5f6c-9eff-8fbe6334f755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=0,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41e4b858-0939-54cf-9dda-08263bf19bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349044} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf2b4e0-ef90-544d-9360-0816914a3219" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25384ce4-a891-57c8-b633-4441e98187a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-81825" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}.$ \nWhat are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =3{b}_{3}-{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =4{b}_{2}-5{b}_{1}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & b & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $C$ relative to the basis $B$ are\n\n$$\n[{c}_{1}]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[{c}_{2}]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[{c}_{3}]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4d259b5-915f-51f3-b82a-a4a9da133e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377846} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4211cf-c344-5620-b95d-15c507cdd614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377847} {:answer/id #uuid "f98495bd-241d-5544-a4a3-84ecc92ef750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377848} {:answer/id #uuid "81b2d894-9407-5098-bffb-afc7c02dc3c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377849} {:answer/id #uuid "af02a450-6787-5267-a45c-7a4ff98dfd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4d259b5-915f-51f3-b82a-a4a9da133e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377846} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78e9f40-0718-5bad-956f-0b197faf152e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43d15cca-f579-50c3-b1dc-880062749688" :question/math-academy-id "q-81850" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & a \\\\ b & -2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & c & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1},{e}_{2},$ and ${e}_{3},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26ea652b-6e02-523c-b2ba-b95e1959da46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=8,$ and $c=5.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416534} {:answer/id #uuid "6615ba74-1fb7-56ad-903d-4b77c16b3dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=7,$ $b=5,$ and $c=8.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416550} {:answer/id #uuid "6c73720e-ba56-5223-a2a5-3a7a43ab5d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=8,$ $b=5,$ and $c=7.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416552} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0f28c3-3134-5225-add6-826343463b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=7,$ and $c=5.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416557} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc6f90c-1011-5415-bdbb-61b071aed3be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=8,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=7.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c73720e-ba56-5223-a2a5-3a7a43ab5d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=8,$ $b=5,$ and $c=7.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416552} :answer-field/id #uuid "8192819f-c781-5fbc-98ce-44030ec1bb27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a2d831b-a3f1-5313-8aee-60f8e3a93edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-81852" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & a & 7 \\\\ -1 & 4 & b \\\\ 4 & c & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1},{e}_{2},$ and ${e}_{3},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=8,$ $b=5,$ and $c=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01517329-3e28-5805-9aac-584f93b1c3a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -7 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348463} {:answer/id #uuid "7366ef02-a1d3-52aa-8a52-8f56ad1cbc7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349508} {:answer/id #uuid "7953ac21-87d0-5f72-9103-11cc5e921515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349566} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d93798-cb62-5237-98c5-12c645b92461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350337} {:answer/id #uuid "e43d5839-5161-5f91-9c65-2cdc6114d39b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7366ef02-a1d3-52aa-8a52-8f56ad1cbc7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349508} :answer-field/id #uuid "6039748f-1eb1-57b4-8f7e-0fdd49400251" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cf13df2-1351-5dd8-a600-8ae95cd08849" :question/math-academy-id "q-81855" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}].$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-5)-(-7)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172c82bc-392e-5ec7-9deb-ddba9ffa9f17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416529} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c0465-d9d9-5b77-b798-d3d852db486e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416551} {:answer/id #uuid "6c8d2ddf-0d76-50dd-b764-f107e2c723e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416553} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb38a3d-724f-59ef-90ac-504aa9672e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416554} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f776f7-8d29-51a7-9c76-2fe228c44b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c8d2ddf-0d76-50dd-b764-f107e2c723e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416553} :answer-field/id #uuid "74971671-dff2-5641-9515-43efb29ddf1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1424ed2-0052-5fff-908d-b6551bfbb11a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81857" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-6)\\cdot 4-(-5)\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070ecd32-2b28-56a5-85bd-6a34995dde85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377853} {:answer/id #uuid "d38da1ce-9f22-5dc7-9d87-db349fd71d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377854} {:answer/id #uuid "0b419e01-bc76-5a21-9ceb-6f1d6f2a07cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377855} {:answer/id #uuid "9298715d-b632-5fb7-a00c-0b61f088dbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377856} {:answer/id #uuid "82445d41-0f67-53ce-9020-32e4dd244a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 & -15 \\\\ -15 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9298715d-b632-5fb7-a00c-0b61f088dbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377856} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab9e6ef9-e9ea-5c1f-ad73-6228ba8a6770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "89bdb866-da97-5894-836a-c2ba9ba48e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-81910" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
